00:29:30,732 --> 00:29:34,048
Speaker 1: In terms of, you know, cultural appropriation or anything, I don't know.

370
00:29:36,312 --> 00:29:39,850
Speaker 1: Like, I don't know enough about their history, 'cause I've not studied that.

371
00:29:41,175 --> 00:29:43,670
Speaker 1: I only know things that I've read on the internet.

372
00:29:43,890 --> 00:29:47,170
Speaker 0: Almost no depiction of them in these old games has their hat on either.

373
00:29:48,331 --> 00:29:48,531
Speaker 1: Yeah.

374
00:29:49,333 --> 00:29:57,410
Speaker 1: So, you know, this person catalogued them on a website, much like I catalogued.

375
00:29:58,631 --> 00:30:00,102
Speaker 1: They did a much better job than I did.

376
00:30:00,162 --> 00:30:01,150
Speaker 1: I only got a few meats.

377
00:30:01,972 --> 00:30:03,599
Speaker 0: It's a pretty good Infinite Scroll catalog.

378
00:30:04,081 --> 00:30:05,930
Speaker 0: I wonder, are there more of these or meats?

379
00:30:07,512 --> 00:30:09,684
Speaker 1: There might be more of these than meats.

380
00:30:09,704 --> 00:30:10,709
Speaker 1: There's so many of them.

381
00:30:11,330 --> 00:30:14,030
Speaker 0: I'm scrolling and scrolling, and it keeps loading.

382
00:30:15,173 --> 00:30:16,546
Speaker 1: Oh yeah, there's so many.

383
00:30:20,313 --> 00:30:21,884
Speaker 1: Good job, moai.games.

384
00:30:23,314 --> 00:30:24,269
Speaker 1: In the meta moment!

385
00:30:25,190 --> 00:30:28,150
Speaker 0: The Geek Nights Book Club book is The Night is Short, Walk on Girl.

386
00:30:28,370 --> 00:30:32,130
Speaker 0: As made famous in nerd circles by the anime, The Night is Short, Walk on Girl.

387
00:30:33,050 --> 00:30:34,058
Speaker 0: I have started reading it.

388
00:30:34,078 --> 00:30:35,308
Speaker 0: Scott has also started reading it.

389
00:30:35,811 --> 00:30:37,089
Speaker 0: Do you wanna show it at some point?

390
00:30:37,550 --> 00:30:41,269
Speaker 0: The previous book club book, The Murderbot Diaries, I'll just mention because we did a show on it.

391
00:30:42,531 --> 00:30:49,750
Speaker 0: There's such short reads, and they're coming up increasingly in my social circles, frequently.

392
00:30:50,751 --> 00:30:55,850
Speaker 0: Those books are being talked about, so you should probably read The Murderbot Diaries if you listen to Geek Nights.

393
00:30:56,150 --> 00:30:56,509
Speaker 0: Just saying.

394
00:30:56,710 --> 00:31:03,110
Speaker 1: Yeah, there might be some internet memeage that you didn't realize was Murderbot jokes until you read the books.

395
00:31:03,330 --> 00:31:04,868
Speaker 1: You can read one book, and that'd be great.

396
00:31:05,050 --> 00:31:05,754
Speaker 0: You'll get most of them.

397
00:31:05,975 --> 00:31:07,764
Speaker 0: Read two books, and you'll get 90% of them, I think.

398
00:31:10,923 --> 00:31:11,128
Speaker 0: So yeah.

399
00:31:12,430 --> 00:31:17,629
Speaker 0: We will be at MAGFest in a couple weeks doing not one, but two panels.

400
00:31:18,710 --> 00:31:20,015
Speaker 0: Those panels are reruns of panels.

401
00:31:20,035 --> 00:31:26,290
Speaker 0: we've done on other cons, but MAGFest is often an environment where-- A couple weeks will be there in like nine days.

402
00:31:27,250 --> 00:31:31,670
Speaker 0: So if you're going to MAGFest, let us know, and we'll probably see you around.

403
00:31:32,111 --> 00:31:36,890
Speaker 0: I plan to spend at least 20% of my time in concerts at this MAGFest.

404
00:31:37,272 --> 00:31:40,609
Speaker 0: At least 10% of my time on DDR machines, assuming they are there.

405
00:31:41,373 --> 00:31:48,029
Speaker 1: And the rest-- I'm not going to budget it, I'm just going to roll with it, but I guarantee I will eat at-- Nando's at least once.

406
00:31:48,890 --> 00:31:51,689
Speaker 1: Nando's chicken, and you know.

407
00:31:52,370 --> 00:31:53,189
Speaker 0: It's going to be a good time.

408
00:31:54,231 --> 00:32:00,770
Speaker 0: I've missed conventions, but MAGFest has always been one of my favorite cons to just go and party at, so I look forward to the party.

409
00:32:02,376 --> 00:32:02,908
Speaker 0: Let's get into it!

410
00:32:03,150 --> 00:32:17,549
Speaker 0: At PEX Unplugged, we played a bunch of tabletop games, and one of them, Scott, comes over and says, "Oh, there's this game, and it looks like it's 18XX-style auctions, but it's not a train game, it's got another thing going

411
00:32:17,689 --> 00:32:17,729
Speaker 0: on.".

412
00:32:18,292 --> 00:32:21,290
Speaker 0: And you kept mentioning it, like, for a while.

413
00:32:21,990 --> 00:32:33,670
Speaker 1: Well, because I went through the first look, and I read the rules to the games that looked like they were worth learning, and weren't going to be big and complicated, or require a bunch of players, or long rules explanations.

414
00:32:34,450 --> 00:32:48,970
Speaker 1: And I saw this one, it was called Whale Street, and the art was kind of good, the production was good, and basically, it wouldn't take us long to learn, because it was mostly the same as 18XX train games.

415
00:32:49,571 --> 00:33:16,210
Speaker 1: And it did something that I had long thought of doing, but few had attempted, which is to make an 18XX game, but to have a simpler, more streamlined operating round, but with that same inside-outside structure, so that you get the same sort of metagame thing, the core, that's the real meat of 18XX in my eyes.

416
00:33:16,491 --> 00:33:24,610
Speaker 1: A lot of people, I think, the core of 18XX for them is the train part, whereas for me, it's the separation of the stock round from the operating round is the core part.

417
00:33:25,130 --> 00:33:35,490
Speaker 1: So, this game replaced the train part entirely with a much simpler game of Texas Hold'em-ish poker.

418
00:33:35,770 --> 00:33:52,941
Speaker 0: Basically, you got companies that will generate poker hands, where some players might know a little more about what that company's up to by having seen a card or two, and you're otherwise playing a pretty straight 18XX stock round

419
00:33:53,483 --> 00:33:54,204
Speaker 1: around

420
00:33:54,224 --> 00:34:02,030
Speaker 0: the poker hands these companies are doing, and the stocks move up or down based on the market of buying and selling, and also based on the poker hands.

421
00:34:02,811 --> 00:34:10,206
Speaker 1: Yep, so instead of companies having this, "Oh, that company's clearly going to be the winner.

422
00:34:10,246 --> 00:34:11,989
Speaker 1: Look how strong it is with capitalism

423
00:34:12,010 --> 00:34:12,170
Speaker 1: snowball."

424
00:34:13,889 --> 00:34:16,476
Speaker 1: It's, "Oh, which company has the best poker

425
00:34:16,496 --> 00:34:16,697
Speaker 1: hand?".

426
00:34:16,717 --> 00:34:21,889
Speaker 1: Well, A, we don't have full information because it's poker and it's not trains where we can see everything.

427
00:34:22,630 --> 00:34:26,909
Speaker 0: And someone runs the company and might make decisions, just like in an 18XX game.

428
00:34:27,291 --> 00:34:32,570
Speaker 1: Someone who's operating a company can choose, just like 18XX, how that company spends its money.

429
00:34:32,670 --> 00:34:45,686
Speaker 1: And now they can choose, "Well, do I spend this company's money to generate a better poker hand to make its stock price go up, or do I spend that company's money on revenues to give me a bunch of money to buy

430
00:34:45,706 --> 00:34:45,826
Speaker 1: others?".

431
00:34:47,090 --> 00:34:50,228
Speaker 0: Or do I just fucking tank the company and sell it all and dump it on someone else?

432
00:34:50,953 --> 00:34:55,150
Speaker 1: Right, it's like there's so many things you can, you know, there's choices to be made there.

433
00:34:56,670 --> 00:35:02,810
Speaker 1: And companies won't snowball so much because your stock price can go back down quite easily.

434
00:35:03,791 --> 00:35:05,877
Speaker 1: And, you know, they have a good hand.

435
00:35:06,058 --> 00:35:09,869
Speaker 1: It's like, "Well, yeah, you had a good hand then, but that doesn't guarantee having a good hand next

436
00:35:09,909 --> 00:35:10,150
Speaker 1: time.".

437
00:35:10,250 --> 00:35:15,948
Speaker 1: Whereas once a company gets its trains going, it's probably going to keep its trains, you know, the companies in 18XX games.

438
00:35:16,690 --> 00:35:18,415
Speaker 1: They get off good, they stay good.

439
00:35:18,877 --> 00:35:20,682
Speaker 1: They get the good tokens, the good tracks.

440
00:35:22,106 --> 00:35:23,370
Speaker 1: You know which ones are going to be good.

441
00:35:23,590 --> 00:35:25,997
Speaker 1: Here, companies can have a bit more up and down.

442
00:35:26,860 --> 00:35:30,170
Speaker 1: It's not a locked-in kind of deal, and that makes it a bit more interesting.

443
00:35:31,030 --> 00:35:34,619
Speaker 1: And so based on that concept, I was like, "We gotta play this game.

444
00:35:34,679 --> 00:35:36,604
Speaker 1: Look, they lock that concept down.

445
00:35:37,426 --> 00:35:39,210
Speaker 1: 18XX but simpler with poker.

446
00:35:39,750 --> 00:35:40,817
Speaker 1: That's a genius thought.

447
00:35:40,837 --> 00:35:42,327
Speaker 1: Whoever thought of it, let's play

448
00:35:42,368 --> 00:35:42,509
Speaker 1: this.".

449
00:35:42,730 --> 00:35:42,870
Speaker 0: Yup.

450
00:35:43,352 --> 00:35:47,462
Speaker 0: And, unfortunately, really, no disrespect to the designers.

451
00:35:47,542 --> 00:35:48,906
Speaker 0: It's well-designed.

452
00:35:48,966 --> 00:35:50,530
Speaker 0: It's got a lot of positive aspects.

453
00:35:51,290 --> 00:36:13,630
Speaker 0: But I'd say over the course of the 30-ish minutes we spent with a group of people who were invested in the concept, learning to play, having a lot of rules clarifications, and then attempting to play, we bounced off of it shockingly hard compared to how enthusiastic we all were coming in.

454
00:36:14,652 --> 00:36:18,450
Speaker 0: This is the first time in a long time I've bounced off of a game unexpectedly.

455
00:36:20,111 --> 00:36:30,580
Speaker 1: Well, I think the hardest, the biggest flaw of the game is that it was not trivial to determine, "Okay, everyone did all their operating.

456
00:36:30,600 --> 00:36:33,770
Speaker 1: They messed with the cards as much as they could.

457
00:36:34,211 --> 00:36:39,048
Speaker 1: We know what the cards are out in the market, the shared cards, because it's a Hold'em style

458
00:36:39,068 --> 00:36:39,410
Speaker 1: poker.".

459
00:36:39,570 --> 00:36:41,803
Speaker 0: We've resolved every rules question.

460
00:36:41,944 --> 00:36:43,070
Speaker 0: Everything is going fine.

461
00:36:43,810 --> 00:36:49,508
Speaker 1: And then the question was, "Okay, which company has the strongest poker

462
00:36:49,548 --> 00:36:49,749
Speaker 1: hand?".

463
00:36:50,412 --> 00:36:53,850
Speaker 1: And answering that question was shockingly difficult.

464
00:36:53,970 --> 00:36:56,389
Speaker 0: Now, when we say shockingly difficult, we messed it up.

465
00:36:56,831 --> 00:37:05,230
Speaker 0: And this is a group of people who I would say are like goddamn experts at the concept of poker hands.

466
00:37:05,510 --> 00:37:05,671
Speaker 1: Right.

467
00:37:05,691 --> 00:37:10,130
Speaker 1: If you're playing poker, it's like actual normal poker, any variety of it.

468
00:37:10,510 --> 00:37:16,330
Speaker 1: You can just reveal your cards and within seconds everyone at the table recognizes that's the winning hand.

469
00:37:16,950 --> 00:37:29,464
Speaker 0: And even if everyone but one person somehow messes it up, like there's 20 people playing Texas Hold'em somehow, at least one person is going to recognize immediately, "Hey, we did this wrong.

470
00:37:29,524 --> 00:37:30,808
Speaker 0: That one wins and it's

471
00:37:30,848 --> 00:37:31,169
Speaker 0: fine.".

472
00:37:31,750 --> 00:37:40,370
Speaker 0: Somehow we messed it up on multiple levels and nobody noticed because everyone was confused.

473
00:37:42,011 --> 00:37:46,170
Speaker 0: And part of this is because the structure of the game is moderately confusing.

474
00:37:46,710 --> 00:37:53,470
Speaker 0: Like there's a lot of confusing steps that really didn't need to be there or didn't seem to serve any game purpose.

475
00:37:54,031 --> 00:38:13,430
Speaker 0: But also instead of using just either a regular deck of cards or a modified but consistent deck of cards, for reasons that I don't really understand, they made their own deck of colors and numbers and also made up their own ranking of what hand is more powerful.

476
00:38:13,530 --> 00:38:18,830
Speaker 0: So if you know poker, you do not necessarily know what makes a stronger hand here.

477
00:38:20,012 --> 00:38:23,025
Speaker 1: So the strongest hand is four of a kind, four of the same number, right?

478
00:38:24,550 --> 00:38:25,660
Speaker 1: So that's easy to spot.

479
00:38:25,740 --> 00:38:26,870
Speaker 1: No one achieved that, right?

480
00:38:27,310 --> 00:38:30,850
Speaker 1: The second strongest hand is like four of the same color, okay?

481
00:38:31,450 --> 00:38:35,650
Speaker 1: And then four numbers in order, so a straight regardless of suit.

482
00:38:36,130 --> 00:38:42,708
Speaker 1: Then three of a kind, three same number, then three same color, then three numbers in order, right?

483
00:38:43,711 --> 00:38:49,430
Speaker 1: And so you go around and you're looking and it's like, aha, I have four numbers in order.

484
00:38:49,650 --> 00:38:53,350
Speaker 1: But wait, I also have four of the same color.

485
00:38:54,695 --> 00:38:57,429
Speaker 1: You have multiples hitting at once because of the shared pool.

486
00:38:58,250 --> 00:39:10,250
Speaker 1: And it's like because it was colors and these numbers and no face cards or anything, it was like, wait, what actually is my hand?

487
00:39:10,350 --> 00:39:15,710
Speaker 1: I thought it was three numbers in order, which I do have, but actually I have three numbers, same color.

488
00:39:15,810 --> 00:39:16,815
Speaker 1: That's what I have.

489
00:39:17,036 --> 00:39:20,210
Speaker 1: And then what does that company have?

490
00:39:21,131 --> 00:39:26,810
Speaker 1: And so it's like, okay, so actually it wasn't the company that we thought that had this.

491
00:39:26,950 --> 00:39:32,790
Speaker 1: It was this other company because they had the same thing, but with a higher number card in there to break the tie.

492
00:39:32,950 --> 00:39:33,822
Speaker 1: We just didn't realize it.

493
00:39:35,072 --> 00:39:47,804
Speaker 0: Couple this with the fact that the rules for when cards flip up, face up so everyone can see them versus remain private, versus when they're in the pool versus when they

494
00:39:47,905 --> 00:39:48,226
Speaker 1: count

495
00:39:48,567 --> 00:39:54,770
Speaker 0: and can be used, we're all just fiddly and you had to keep a lot in your head to figure out the poker hand.

496
00:39:55,250 --> 00:39:59,970
Speaker 0: All the procedures meant that everyone struggled to understand the odds and we messed up the poker hands.

497
00:40:00,592 --> 00:40:03,023
Speaker 0: And that wasn't even the biggest problem.

498
00:40:03,063 --> 00:40:04,690
Speaker 0: That was just the straw that broke the camel's back.

499
00:40:05,170 --> 00:40:10,730
Speaker 0: And suddenly everyone else at the table kind of wilted and all voted to not continue the game.

500
00:40:10,770 --> 00:40:14,330
Speaker 0: And we actually stopped playing after a little more interrogation of the rules.

501
00:40:16,531 --> 00:40:27,390
Speaker 1: I think it's also, it's just, you know, even though the concept was so strong of simplifying the operating round of an 18XX game with something simpler and poker, I don't think was a bad choice.

502
00:40:27,510 --> 00:40:29,036
Speaker 0: Poker's a great choice.

503
00:40:29,417 --> 00:40:32,850
Speaker 0: Poker adjacent, there's a lot of ways, that wasn't even the worst.

504
00:40:33,050 --> 00:40:39,250
Speaker 0: But all the problems of the poker hands were weirdly hard to do, that's not insurmountable.

505
00:40:39,770 --> 00:40:51,490
Speaker 0: The method and means of adding, removing, and revealing cards was not predictable enough for player agency to seem to matter that much.

506
00:40:51,550 --> 00:40:58,110
Speaker 0: Like the value of the companies felt like it was a lot more random than it needed to be.

507
00:40:58,771 --> 00:41:03,402
Speaker 1: Well, I don't think it's how random it was.

508
00:41:03,503 --> 00:41:04,184
Speaker 1: That didn't matter.

509
00:41:04,265 --> 00:41:06,350
Speaker 1: I think what matters is that it was random.

510
00:41:06,470 --> 00:41:07,740
Speaker 0: Well, it was really random.

511
00:41:07,781 --> 00:41:08,950
Speaker 0: It was more like betting a lot of hits.

512
00:41:08,970 --> 00:41:09,734
Speaker 1: But it doesn't matter how random.

513
00:41:09,915 --> 00:41:13,070
Speaker 1: Any randomness at all would have been too much, right?

514
00:41:13,170 --> 00:41:18,346
Speaker 1: We forgot when seeing the concept of, aha, simplified 18XX operating.

515
00:41:18,366 --> 00:41:19,490
Speaker 1: Terrific, right?

516
00:41:19,731 --> 00:41:28,910
Speaker 1: We forget the other core thing of 18XX, which is that there's really no randomness other than who goes first and maybe randomize the private companies to start.

517
00:41:28,930 --> 00:41:35,550
Speaker 0: At the very least, players in the stock round can make reasonable valuation assessments of the companies.

518
00:41:35,910 --> 00:41:43,330
Speaker 0: Here, honestly, it was more like playing an 18XX game, and the companies are effectively betting on three different slot machines.

519
00:41:45,152 --> 00:41:46,697
Speaker 0: And that is not a satisfying game.

520
00:41:46,958 --> 00:41:50,490
Speaker 0: But to make it worse, each company has a built-in power.

521
00:41:50,710 --> 00:41:53,445
Speaker 0: Now, before you think, oh, some 18XX games have that.

522
00:41:53,526 --> 00:41:54,129
Speaker 0: Yeah, they do.

523
00:41:54,910 --> 00:42:07,590
Speaker 0: But these powers were all very different from each other and extremely fiddly to the point that everyone had to keep reading them over and over and over again and then checking to see if they applied or not.

524
00:42:07,790 --> 00:42:11,821
Speaker 0: Like, they were not well-written or designed.

525
00:42:11,881 --> 00:42:15,170
Speaker 0: Like, I would have removed that whole concept or radically simplified it.

526
00:42:16,151 --> 00:42:17,714
Speaker 0: Like, they were just deeply unsatisfying.

527
00:42:17,754 --> 00:42:25,050
Speaker 0: Like, Shell Motors, it has some arbitrary limits and some stuff I don't really want to explain because it's too fiddly.

528
00:42:25,451 --> 00:42:34,850
Speaker 0: But its power is at phase three, if the sum of the tech cards on the market board is 12 or less, this company gets eight revenue.

529
00:42:35,652 --> 00:42:36,273
Speaker 0: So every

530
00:42:36,293 --> 00:42:36,674
Speaker 1: time any

531
00:42:36,774 --> 00:42:49,725
Speaker 0: fucking thing happens in the game, everyone has to look and check, is that true or not, to then determine whether or not this company will have eight more dollars, which will then impact what that company is capable of during its operating round, which determines its valuation.

532
00:42:50,246 --> 00:42:51,389
Speaker 0: Nobody's got time for that.

533
00:42:54,271 --> 00:42:59,920
Speaker 1: But I think you can't have an 18XX simplifying the operating round with some other game.

534
00:43:00,260 --> 00:43:06,470
Speaker 1: that reduces how much time the game takes, reduces complexity, but keeps the core is a great idea.

535
00:43:07,031 --> 00:43:15,850
Speaker 1: But you have to keep the other thing that's true about 18XX, which is that there's really no hidden information and there's really no randomness.

536
00:43:19,211 --> 00:43:30,930
Speaker 0: I would accept a tiny amount of randomness in that I would be okay with the president of a company knowing an X1 card that isn't public right away.

537
00:43:31,750 --> 00:43:39,470
Speaker 1: I mean, yeah, it's obviously more randomness would be more damaging and less would be much better, but zero is really ideal for this kind of game.

538
00:43:40,611 --> 00:43:45,490
Speaker 1: In a real stock market, if someone has an information advantage, they just win.

539
00:43:46,190 --> 00:43:48,894
Speaker 0: The cards in the pool would move too fast.

540
00:43:49,114 --> 00:43:58,988
Speaker 0: Cards would come in quickly and players who ran companies could take actions to push cards through the pool even faster, which added randomness multiplied by randomness.

541
00:44:03,393 --> 00:44:13,430
Speaker 0: And it was really unsatisfying to play and the rules were so fiddly that we spent a long time continually referring to the rules to figure out how to do basic procedural things.

542
00:44:14,170 --> 00:44:23,430
Speaker 0: And then to make matters worse, the physical components, if you so much as bump the table, you will ruin the game because you will not know what anything costs anymore.

543
00:44:24,172 --> 00:44:26,196
Speaker 0: The spaces to put things were too small.

544
00:44:26,216 --> 00:44:32,630
Speaker 0: Lots of little physical design flaws kind of sealed the deal on us bouncing off of this game, unfortunately.

545
00:44:32,830 --> 00:44:39,430
Speaker 1: Yeah, I think they were trying to keep the game small, like physically small, so it wasn't some giant box full of enormous things.

546
00:44:40,350 --> 00:44:45,570
Speaker 0: But there is a reason 18XX games are huge and it is not just because they're train games.

547
00:44:48,391 --> 00:44:51,336
Speaker 1: The smallness saves the money on production.

548
00:44:52,057 --> 00:44:55,944
Speaker 1: B, it makes the game look approachable.

549
00:44:56,024 --> 00:44:57,307
Speaker 1: There isn't that much game here.

550
00:44:57,327 --> 00:44:59,250
Speaker 1: There's only a few types of components.

551
00:45:01,410 --> 00:45:05,238
Speaker 0: There's actually a lot of game here, but it's not the part you think it is.

552
00:45:05,278 --> 00:45:11,590
Speaker 0: It's like an 18XX game, if the operating rounds were not only random, but ten times more complex.

553
00:45:12,652 --> 00:45:16,730
Speaker 0: And then the stock rounds were also more complex, but none of that worked.

554
00:45:17,850 --> 00:45:20,595
Speaker 1: Right, it is simpler than an 18XX game.

555
00:45:21,035 --> 00:45:29,930
